ove 0.9  however there is a positive proportional  bias of approximately 15 percent in the  kappa  and a negative proportional bias of 20  in the lambda  there also appears to be a constant bias  of approximately 0.3 milligrams per  deciliter in the lambda  another thing we want to examine in the  study was the issue of reference ranges  shown here are the reference ranges for  free kappa free lambda  and the capital lambda ratio established  at the mayo clinic using free light  reagents  also shown is the 100 diagnostic range  for the kappa lambda ratio  which has been shown to be indicative of  the presence of a monolinguality when a  patient has a value outside the range  this range is currently used in clinical  practice worldwide for the purposes of  screening patients for monoclonal  hemopsis  as well as managing myeloma patients  [Music]  in this slide we show the reference  ranges we obtain for measuring 127  presumed healthy donors  based on the central 95 percentile we  find that within our local population  the reference range using the binding  site assay for free kappa is 0.84 to 2.7  milligrams per deciliter and 0.75 to  2.52 milligrams per deciliter for free  lambda  the reference range using the diazimasta  is 0.76 to 3.19 milligrams per deciliter  for kappa  and 0.19 to 1.65 milligrams per  deciliter for lambda  the reference range is proposed by  diazyme and their package insert  is 0.24 to 2.07 milligrams per deciliter  for kappa  and 0.42 to 2.77 milligrams per  deciliter  for lambda  this slide shows the comparison of the  reference range for the kappa to lambda  ratio  obtained by taking the central 95  percent of the values obtained  in the study based on this method the  values obtained from the free light  method  gave a range of 0.66 to 1.82  while the diazyme assay gave a range of  1.1 to 9.96  another interesting thing to note is  that the reference is that the range of  capital  lambda ratios for all 127 donors  with 0.59 to 1.9 for the free light  ratio  while the range using the diazyme acid  give a very wide range of 0.98 to 56.50  these high ratios will be considered  highly abnormal and diagnostic of the  presence  of a monocle engamopti if obtained using  the binding site free light assay  based on the widely accepted 100  diagnostic range for the kappa lambda  ratio  we found that nine of the 127 healthy  donors will be classified as abnormal  if we applied the same criteria i.e the  capital lambda ratio of 0.26 to 1.65  to the value of the values obtained from  the diazime assay  we find that 106 of the 127 donors will  be classified as abnormal  in addition we found that 102 out of 127  donors fell outside the reference range  recommended  for the capital lambda ratio by diazine  which is 0.22 to 1.74  when using the diazyme assay it should  be noted that diazyme currently does not  provide 100  diagnostic range for their assay thus  this is an unequal comparison comparing  the number of patients  falling outside a reference range using  the diazyme assay  with the number of patients falling  outside a diagnostic range  however it is apparent the significant  adjustments will have to be made by  clinical staff  and resolved interpretation when making  a switch from binding site  to diazyme reagents  our conclusions based on this  preliminary study is as follows  use of the diazyme assay will require  the laboratory to develop their own  dilution protocols to analyze specimens  with elevated kappa orlando levels  this is particularly important as free  light chains have a very wide dynamic  range  among patients with myeloma and other  monoclonal homopathies  further dilution may be required even  when the instrument gives a numerical  result as the resulting numbers  may potentially be falsely low  significant discrepancies can be noted  in kappa and lambda quantitation  particularly at high concentrations  significant discordance is seen in kappa  lambda ratios between the binding site  and diazyme assays  both in patients and healthy donors  and finally any medical decision points  based on capital lambda ratios will have  to be redetermined in separate studies  for the divine assay  i would like to end this presentation  with some short take-home messages based  on our experience comparing the two free  light chain assays  it is very important for laboratories to  perform their own validation using  clinical specimens prior to implementing  a new free light chain assay  also free light chain assays as of  present are not identical to each other  nor are they standardized  we believe that caution is warranted  whenever our laboratory is considering a  switch  between free-like chain assays thank you  very much and i'll be glad to take any  questions at this time  thank you dr morata for that informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen and we'll answer as  many questions as we have time for  so let's take a look we already have  some great questions  coming in dr morata can you elaborate  more on the conclusion point that says  further dilution may be required even  when  instrument gives a numerical result as  the resulting number  may potentially be falsely low does this  mean  there are issues with antigen excess  well we we believe that oxygen excess is  probably the most likely cause we did  not specifically you know save the  specimens to do further analysis um  because the way that we did this study  we essentially set up the  instruments based on the instructions  provided by the manufacturer and the  um abbott was the one who set up the  essay for us  um but essentially if the instrument  gave an error  we didn't necessarily we probably should  have done a manual dilution to  determine that it was antigenexis but if  we were to guess it probably is going to  be an antigen excess  as we did an analysis of the we looked  at the specimens that didn't give an  um a numerical value  and that yielded a lot of high uh  values for free like chains especially  for the lambda  so we do suspect that it might  potentially be an antigen excess  yes and and how did you  how did your findings compare to results  from other diazyme evaluations that have  been published  so as far as i'm aware now there may  potentially be more  by now when i last did the literature  search there were two studies that have  been published  one uh by alan wu ucsf and then  another i believe at um from louisville  um and they they have both um sort of  shown that  the uh assays are relatively comparable  but the one thing i want to add is that  at the major cancer center we did a  fairly extensive  evaluation of both healthy and  patients with monoclonal homopathies and  so i we believe that  it's a lot more thorough than what they  did which is just simply  see if the values were relatively  similar we did an analysis including the  ratios  and the clinical significance um and one  thing that's very important with the  capital lambda  analysis is the actual ratio and so when  you do these on  patients and with healthy um donors  you do end up you kind of see where the  um issues are with the um  other assays and so i i think that  we have shown a little bit we kind of  dug a little bit deeper and we  did find that there are some aspects  where  um they're not entirely comparable um  and so we do strongly recommend that  people  um or laboratories do their own  validations um instead of  in thorough evaluations not just ones  that are required from a  clia point of view but clinical  evaluations on real patients to make  sure that  what they're getting both for the  quantitation and the ratios  are similar  thank you so much and i want to thank  our audience again for these great  questions  coming in our next question an audience  member says more than a hun