ay is that  um so the binding site actually controls  um  specifically what instrument it's run  it's run on for and there's  actually probably now that we've run it  on the abbott  instruments i think it kind of makes  sense  the if you're not careful with the  bindingside assay you can  end up with antigen excess as well too  and i published on this before but those  are relatively rare and the reason why  is because  they actually do have an instrument  called the um  [Music]  i tried blanking on the name it's the um  it used to be the binding site spa but  now it's the um  uh optilite and the the opulent is  actually programmed  specifically to prevent the antigen  excess  and so all the binding site assays are  run  on the optilite which is specifically  programmed  for running the binding site assays and  also to prevent  the antigen excess issues  that could potentially be encountered  thank you so much dr morada we have time  for one more question  for error messages exceeding amr  were they above or below amr  [Music]  we believe that they were probably above  the amr it's just that  i think with when you run the essay on  these sort of other third party um  manufacturers sometimes it's  because there's other um  for example on the when you run the free  light on the optilite there are  mechanisms in place to prevent  to detect um the antigen excess  when you run assays on other platforms  such as  the architect or other third party um  instruments there is no specific  mechanism to detect it so  even though a result may be outside  the amr you don't all the machine might  potentially falsely detect it as within  the amr  by antigen excess and so  we believe that the result that we got  probably were outside the amr it just  wasn't detected as being  outside the amr um because it just  didn't have the sort of safety  mechanisms in place to prevent  um the machine from  letting a result through without  recognizing the potential antigen access  [Music]  dr m